1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,806
Have you ever had a long Claude Code session, where Claude suddenly forgot something you told it at the start?

2
00:00:06,256 --> 00:00:12,126
That's the context window at work. In this video, you'll learn what it is, and how to manage it like a pro.

3
00:00:12,576 --> 00:00:18,731
I'll explain how it works, when it causes problems, and then show you four practical habits, step by step.

4
00:00:20,281 --> 00:00:21,809
So, what is the context window?

5
00:00:22,259 --> 00:00:30,114
It's Claude's working memory. Everything Claude can see right now: your messages, the files it read, and the output of the commands it ran.

6
00:00:30,564 --> 00:00:39,704
Some things load automatically, before you even type: Claude Code's instructions, your CLAUDE.md file, memory, and the names of your skills and tools.

7
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:46,965
And it has a limit, measured in tokens. Most models give you two hundred thousand tokens. Some offer up to a million.

8
00:00:48,415 --> 00:00:56,601
A token is a small piece of text: a short word, or part of a longer one. Two hundred thousand tokens is roughly a few hundred pages.

9
00:00:57,051 --> 00:01:03,966
That sounds like a lot. But big files, long test output, and long conversations fill it up surprisingly fast.

10
00:01:05,416 --> 00:01:06,786
Here's what happens as you work.

11
00:01:07,236 --> 00:01:12,803
First, the window fills up. Every file read, and every command output, takes space.

12
00:01:13,253 --> 00:01:23,170
Second, near the limit, Claude Code compacts automatically. It clears older tool output first, then summarizes the conversation. So your session keeps going.

13
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:30,072
But third: a summary is shorter than the original. Detailed instructions from early in the chat can get lost.

14
00:01:30,522 --> 00:01:35,577
That's why rules that must last belong in a CLAUDE.md file, not just in the chat.

15
00:01:37,027 --> 00:01:43,225
Let's see the problem. At the start of this session, I told Claude: always run the tests after every change.

16
00:01:43,675 --> 00:01:49,772
Two hours later, after dozens of file reads and test runs, the context window is getting full.

17
00:01:50,222 --> 00:01:58,779
Now I ask for another change. Claude edits the file, and stops. No tests. After compaction, my early instruction didn't survive.

18
00:01:59,229 --> 00:02:01,579
Let's fix that, with four simple habits.

19
00:02:02,429 --> 00:02:05,230
Habit one: see what's inside your context window.

20
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:11,948
Type slash context. You get a live breakdown of what's using space, by category, with suggestions.

21
00:02:12,398 --> 00:02:18,388
Here, the conversation itself is by far the biggest part: all those file reads and test runs.

22
00:02:18,838 --> 00:02:23,847
The numbers in your own session will be different. The point is to check, before the window fills up.

23
00:02:24,697 --> 00:02:28,106
Habit two: save lasting rules in CLAUDE.md.

24
00:02:28,556 --> 00:02:37,284
CLAUDE.md is loaded at the start of every session. And after compaction, Claude Code reloads it from disk. So rules here don't get lost.

25
00:02:37,734 --> 00:02:41,071
I move my test rule here, plus a couple of others I always want.

26
00:02:41,521 --> 00:02:49,635
And I add a Compact instructions section. It tells Claude what to keep when it summarizes: test output, code changes, and failing tests.

27
00:02:50,485 --> 00:02:52,847
Habit three: compact with a focus.

28
00:02:53,297 --> 00:03:00,340
Claude Code compacts on its own near the limit. But before a big new step, you can do it yourself, and say what matters.

29
00:03:00,790 --> 00:03:07,433
The summary keeps what you chose, instead of what the automatic pass guesses is important. And you get lots of free space back.

30
00:03:08,283 --> 00:03:10,471
Habit four: clear between tasks.

31
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:17,446
When you switch to unrelated work, old conversation just gets in the way, and costs tokens on every message.

32
00:03:17,896 --> 00:03:23,236
So I give this session a name with slash rename, then type slash clear, to start fresh.

33
00:03:23,686 --> 00:03:30,296
A clean context window, for the next task. And I can come back to the old session any time, with slash resume.

34
00:03:30,746 --> 00:03:38,267
Now, with the rule in CLAUDE.md, the same request ends the right way: Claude edits the file, and runs the tests.

35
00:03:39,817 --> 00:03:42,560
Step five: go further. Four more tips.

36
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:51,934
One: delegate big jobs, like test runs or research, to a subagent. The long output stays in its own context window, not yours.

37
00:03:52,384 --> 00:03:59,149
Two: disable plugins and MCP servers you don't use. Their descriptions load into every session.

38
00:03:59,599 --> 00:04:04,376
Three: run slash context during long sessions, before the window fills up.

39
00:04:04,826 --> 00:04:10,681
And four: if you really need more room, some models support a one million token context window.

40
00:04:12,231 --> 00:04:12,885
Let's recap.

41
00:04:13,335 --> 00:04:17,632
The context window is Claude's working memory: everything it can see right now.

42
00:04:18,082 --> 00:04:23,408
When it fills up, Claude Code compacts the conversation, and early details can get lost.

43
00:04:23,858 --> 00:04:31,303
So check it with slash context, keep lasting rules in CLAUDE.md, compact with a focus, and clear between tasks.

44
00:04:32,753 --> 00:04:37,407
The CLAUDE.md example and a one-page cheat sheet are linked in the description.

45
00:04:37,857 --> 00:04:44,688
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

